        "summary":  "A Harvard Business School, MIT, and University of Washington study found that when AI systems provide written explanations for their recommendations, human evaluators are more likely to defer to incorrect AI decisions rather than independently verify them. Paradoxically, black-box AI recommendations without explanations produced better decision quality than narrative explanations, as the latter suppress productive human disagreement. The research identified cognitive biases and the \u0027illusion of explanatory depth\u0027 as key mechanisms driving this effect.",
        "execSummary":  "This research reveals a critical paradox in human-AI collaboration: AI-provided explanations do not necessarily improve decision quality and may actively harm it, particularly for high-stakes decisions where false negatives carry strategic consequences. When AI provides fluent rationales, humans tend to passively accept its recommendations due to cognitive biases — abandoning independent judgment precisely when it is most needed. Enterprise leaders must reconsider how AI recommendation systems are designed, specifically whether transparent explanations are appropriate for decisions where human disagreement and challenge are valuable. Organizations deploying AI for consequential decisions should audit whether their interface design inadvertently suppresses the critical thinking they need their teams to apply.",
